rns on the secondary  coil than the primary coil so I can just  by looking at this I can tell this is  step-up transformer because there's more  turns on the secondary than on the  primary okay whenever we have a  constantly changing magnetic field we  actually induce a current in this wire  here a changing magnetic field induces a  current in this one here again it's  going to be an alternating current  because we have a constantly reversing  magnetic field because we have more  turns on this coil here than on this  coil here our voltage is going to  increase so let's have a quick look at  the structure of our step-down  transformer we got similar kind of idea  but in Reverse so here's just an example  of step-down could transformer now the  difference for the step-down transformer  is it's just the opposite again we have  alternating current but this time we  have more turns on the primary coil  compared to the secondary coil so this  time round as the electricity passes  through here we establish a magnetic  field because it's alternating current  that current is going to create a  constantly changing magnetic field  throughout this iron core this changing  magnetic field is going to induce a  current in the wire on our secondary  coil and because we've got more terms on  our primary than our secondary it's  going to step down the potential  difference ie  decrease the potential difference so  that is how transformers work they  change the potential difference a  step-up transformer increases potential  difference in order to minimize the  amount of energy loss as we transport  the electricity over long distances a  step-down transformer  decreases the potential difference so  it's safe for that voltage that  potential difference to be used in our  homes thank you
 

